<interrogationes unitas="aedilis">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build and run a program or test without writing a build script?
    <responsum imperium="./bin/aedilis probationes/probatio_stml.c --currere" via="tools/aedilis.c">Run
      bin/aedilis on the file that has main, from the repository root:
      it derives the closure, writes build/aedilis/BASIS/manifestum.stml
      and a bash script, and with --currere runs the script, which
      leaves the binary at build/aedilis/BASIS/BASIS. --varians picks a
      platform variant; --enumerare (print the objects) and --ordo
      (print the headers in dependency order) print and exit without
      writing the manifest or the script. If derivation fails it
      refuses loudly (AEDILIS RECUSAT on stderr, exit 1, cause named)
      and writes nothing; with --currere a failing script also gives
      exit 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I derive a closure from C code instead of the command line?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_configurationem_legere aedilis_derivare aedilis_silva_creare aedilis_silva_extrahere">Read
      aedilis.stml with aedilis_configurationem_legere, make the real
      extractor with aedilis_silva_creare, and call aedilis_derivare
      with aedilis_silva_extrahere and that extractor as its datum
      (radix_thesauri NIHIL runs without the cross-run cache; with a
      cache, the key comes from aedilis_thesauri_praefixum). The engine
      itself never touches silva: the extractor is injected, so tests
      pass a fixture extractor instead. aedilis_configurationem_legere
      and aedilis_derivare return NIHIL and fill *causa_out on refusal;
      there is never a partial result. aedilis_silva_creare has no
      causa_out: it just returns NIHIL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what object file does aedilis pick for a header I include?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_derivare">By
      convention: include/x.h maps to lib/x, any other header to its own
      directory. For each preferred variant in order (only the given one
      with --varians, otherwise the praelatio of aedilis.stml, macos
      then posix) it tries x_VARIANT.m, then x_VARIANT.c; then plain
      x.c. A plain x.m is never tried: declare it with a corpus
      annotation. A header with none of these simply has no object. An
      irregularia rule in aedilis.stml replaces the convention for that
      header.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what annotations does aedilis read from source comments?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_derivare" via="include/modulus.h">Elements
      of the form &lt;aedilis VERB="ARG"/&gt; in comments of any file the
      derivation walks (so never in an unwalked object): obiectum (link this source, never walked), corpus (in
      a header: its implementation, walked, must exist), vexillum (a
      LINK flag), nexus (only the value purus), facultas (fenestra, rete
      or repositorium; scope file only) and instrumentum (a bin/ binary
      the scope runs; scope file only, under bin/, no duplicates, no
      ..). Any other verb or value, or obiectum/corpus/vexillum with an
      empty argument, refuses the whole derivation.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are the includes of some objects never walked?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_derivare">Two
      cases are compiled and linked but not walked. Objects declared by
      an obiectum annotation: their dependencies belong to their
      generator (parsing 9 MB of generated capsules took 17 s for
      nothing). And sources over 1 MB, which are treated as data written
      as code (a 6.1 MB Bible source made silva run out of memory). A
      declared object missing from disk (absens, e.g. not generated
      yet) is not walked either. Use a corpus annotation when you DO
      want the implementation walked; the 1 MB limit still applies to
      it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the difference between systemata and inresolutae in a manifest?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_derivare aedilis_manifestum_scribere">An
      include that resolves nowhere under the root (as written, beside
      the including file, or under the -I directories of aedilis.stml)
      goes into systemata. If it was written in quoted form it is also
      listed in inresolutae: probably one of our headers that the
      include roots miss, so the manifest is incomplete. Angle-bracket
      includes are only systemata. The inresolutae section is written
      only when it is not empty.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I promise that a header pulls in no framework (pure link)?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_nexum_purum_probare" via="include/modulus.h">Put
      &lt;aedilis nexus="purus"/&gt; in the header's comment.
      aedilis_nexum_purum_probare derives the header as if it were a
      scope and fails, naming the include chain and the flags, if its own
      implementation or any object in its closure has a nexus rule
      (e.g. fenestra_macos -> Cocoa). A header without the promise
      passes. bin/aedilis --nexus-purus checks every header under the
      include roots.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get a bash build script from a derivation?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_scriptum_scribere aedilis_scriptum_scribere_ad">aedilis_scriptum_scribere
      returns the script text. With solitarius FALSUM objects are shared
      in build/aedilis/obiecta and rebuilt when missing, older than their
      source, or older than ANY header of the closure; with VERUM they
      go to a private solitarius directory and are always rebuilt
      (vendor objects excepted: in both modes they are rebuilt only
      when missing or older than their source). Objects are named by flattened path
      (lib/tcp_posix.c becomes lib__tcp_posix.o, with the flags of a
      compilatio rule appended, so lib__sigillum__O2.o). The flattening
      is not one-to-one despite the comment in lib/aedilis.c:
      lib/a__b.c and lib/a/b.c would both become lib__a__b.o. The script must
      run from the repository root. aedilis_scriptum_scribere_ad sends
      outputs and objects to other directories.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a vendored C file get our warning flags?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_scriptum_scribere_ad aedilis_configurationem_legere">No:
      that is deliberate. An include under vendor/ makes X.c beside X.h
      (or the file itself) a vendor object, compiled with ONLY the flags
      of its vendor rule in aedilis.stml: neither the shared VEXILLA
      nor the -I directories. It is rebuilt only when its own source is
      newer. A vendor header without a matching .c is treated as
      header-only.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I list headers so that every header comes after the ones it includes?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_capita_ordinare">aedilis_capita_ordinare
      returns a Xar of AedilisCaput pointers into the derivation (stable,
      because xar never moves elements). Within one round the discovery
      order is kept, so the result is deterministic. An include cycle
      returns NIHIL and causa_out names every header not yet emitted
      (the cycle plus anything that depends on it). Edges
      exist only for headers (a header's inclusa list); implementation
      files can be in any order.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the extractor cache key from aedilis_thesauri_praefixum?
    <responsum symbolum="aedilis_thesauri_praefixum">The
      prefix under which extraction records are cached. When ingressus is
      a valid digest (64 lowercase hex, built into the binary) the key is
      the seal of extractor: plus that digest plus the bytes of
      aedilis.stml, so every binary built from the same extractor sources
      shares the cache. Otherwise (NIHIL, bootstrap, malformed) it seals
      the binary file plus the configuration. *fons_out says which was
      used; FALSUM if a file needed could not be read.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
